WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.720
Mr. Hankey, the Christmas Poo, is probably&nbsp;
the world's most disgusting Christmas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:08.560
icon. But he's also one of South&nbsp;
Park's most recognizable characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:11.600
and has become a bona fide&nbsp;
symbol of the holiday season.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:16.000
We're here to explore the history of&nbsp;
this gross but lovable Christmas mascot.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.480
While it seems like Mr. Hankey, the Christmas Poo&nbsp;
might just be something that the reliably wacky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:25.120
gross, and crude South Park writing staff&nbsp;
squeezed out, the character predates the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:30.160
series by more than two decades. The sentient,&nbsp;
singing stool sample was invented by South&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:36.240
Park co-creator Trey Parker's father in the&nbsp;
early 1970s. As Parker explained to Variety:
 &nbsp;

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:40.080
"When I was three years old, I had a bad&nbsp;
habit of not flushing the toilet after I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:44.720
was finished. My mother asked my father to have&nbsp;
a little 'talk' with me about toilet flushing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:49.680
He told me that if I didn't, 'Mr. Hankey will&nbsp;
come out of the toilet and annoy you forever.'"

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:54.720
In other tellings, Parker has recalled his father&nbsp;
being a little more harsh, insinuating that if he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:59.120
failed to flush, his leavings would transform&nbsp;
into Mr. Hankey, who would then come after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:03.440
little Trey and try to straight-up kill him.&nbsp;
On the one hand, this is one of the grossest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:08.240
ways to traumatize a child into good behavior.&nbsp;
On the other hand, it appears to have worked.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:11.034
"Hiiii-dee-ho!"

00:01:11.034 --> 00:01:14.640
Mr. Hankey makes his entrance in the&nbsp;
middle of the first season of South Park,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:19.120
but has never become much more than a minor,&nbsp;
recurring character on a richly developed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:23.520
well-populated series. This low-key&nbsp;
fate wasn't set in stone, however.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:28.400
Show creators Trey Parker and Matt Stone&nbsp;
initially had much bigger plans for the living,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:32.960
laughing excrement, with Parker recalling&nbsp;
on a South Park DVD commentary track:

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:37.600
"Mr. Hankey was something we wanted to make even&nbsp;
in college. When we were making the South Park&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:40.960
shorts, we always talked about&nbsp;
making a short with Mr. Hankey."

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:45.600
That short film never got made, but some of the&nbsp;
plot points were ultimately used in "Mr. Hankey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:50.560
the Christmas Poo," the 1997 South Park&nbsp;
episode that introduces the character.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:55.200
Stone explained a scene where the poo is revealed&nbsp;
to be alive. In the short that never got made,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:59.840
Mr. Hankey would have just been a figment of&nbsp;
the imagination of a disturbed young child&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:04.640
who smears stuff on the walls and claims his poo&nbsp;
is sentient. The Mr. Hankey that made it to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:09.920
screen is just as gross, but at least his maker,&nbsp;
Kyle, can be afforded a little more respect.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:15.680
South Park's origins lie in two crudely animated&nbsp;
short cartoons called "The Spirit of Christmas"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:20.800
that Trey Parker and Matt Stone made in the&nbsp;
early 1990s. After seeing the first short,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:26.240
in which Jesus fights Frosty the Snowman, Fox&nbsp;
network executive Brian Graden commissioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:30.800
Parker and Stone to make another cartoon he&nbsp;
could use as a Christmas card. After this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:34.160
Parker and Stone took a few meetings&nbsp;
about developing an animated series.

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:36.935
“Behold my glory.”

00:02:36.935 --> 00:02:39.120
"Holy s---, it's Jesus!"

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:42.960
Rather than pitching adventures with the four&nbsp;
little boys from "The Spirit of Christmas",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:48.000
Parker and Stone's initial pitch for a series&nbsp;
was The Mr. Hankey Show, an animated series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:53.120
about the life-imbued piece of fecal matter who&nbsp;
was the focus of their unmade short cartoon.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:58.320
Stan, Kyle, Kenny, and Cartman would have been&nbsp;
supporting characters with Mr. Hankey center&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:03.440
stage, commenting on life and current events.&nbsp;
Graden, Parker, and Stone's contact at Fox,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:08.720
adamantly refused to put a show about excrement on&nbsp;
television, and so the writers heeded some advice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:13.360
they'd been given and instead developed South&nbsp;
Park, a show about four kids living in a strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:19.120
town. The duo found corporate champions in Comedy&nbsp;
Central, where executives expressly told Parker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:24.000
and Stone that they had no problem with Mr. Hankey&nbsp;
-and thus some very strange history was made.

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:30.000
Mr. Hankey debuted in the South Park episode&nbsp;
entitled "Mr. Hankey, the Christmas Poo." First&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.640
airing on December 17, 1997, it was a monumental,&nbsp;
watershed moment in the animated series' history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:40.400
with effects ranging far beyond the&nbsp;
mere introduction of a talking turd.

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:45.520
"Mr. Hankey, the Christmas Poo" is the first of&nbsp;
many Christmas episodes in South Park history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:48.560
and introduces many familiar South Park tunes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:51.760
including Eric Cartman's classic&nbsp;
number, "Kyle's Mom is a Big Fat",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:56.560
and also, of course, Mr. Hankey's own&nbsp;
Christmas carol-style theme song. "Mr. Hankey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:02.000
the Christmas Poo" is also the first ever musical&nbsp;
South Park episode, predating both the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:07.280
South Park: Bigger, Longer, and Uncut, and Parker&nbsp;
and Stone's Broadway hit, The Book of Mormon.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:10.080
Additionally, Mr. Hankey's&nbsp;
arrival coincides with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:14.000
introduction of two other South Park&nbsp;
characters. His very special episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:18.800
marks the debut of troubled kid Craig Tucker,&nbsp;
and that of guidance counselor Mr. Mackey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:23.440
who attempts to convince Kyle that he isn't&nbsp;
really interacting with a talking Christmas poo.

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:28.640
“In your screwed up little head,&nbsp;
he’s the only friend you have. Kyle!”

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:32.400
The holiday spirit of Mr. Hankey is so&nbsp;
strong that it ends up subverting one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:37.040
of South Park's most famous and popular early&nbsp;
tropes. "Mr. Hankey, the Christmas Poo" is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:42.160
first episode where the frequently-killed Kenny&nbsp;
somehow doesn't die. Talk about groundbreaking.

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:47.600
Mr. Hankey's real world origins are rooted in&nbsp;
Trey Parker's childhood, but in the universe of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:52.400
the TV series, the character's beginnings are a&nbsp;
whole lot muddier. In fact, it's actually never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:57.440
quite revealed how Mr. Hankey came to be. Clues&nbsp;
to his origins are spread out over his occasional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:02.880
appearances, which span two decades. They suggest&nbsp;
he's an ancient being of tremendous magical gifts.

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:06.960
In "Red Sleigh Down," he visits the&nbsp;
ageless Santa Claus, and the two appear to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:11.760
be old friends. And while Kyle is the first&nbsp;
person in South Park to discover Mr. Hankey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:15.600
when the magical creature pops out&nbsp;
of his toilet, Chef for some reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:20.080
already knows about the friendly piece of&nbsp;
feces. Mr. Hankey is clearly not just some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:24.800
of Kyle's waste sprung to life, though if you can&nbsp;
believe it, that's still about as much as we know.

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:31.040
Mr. Hankey wasn’t always a holiday character, with&nbsp;
Parker explaining in a South Park DVD commentary:

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:34.720
"I'd actually been drawing Mr. Hankey&nbsp;
since I was a kid, [and I’d] been drawing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:38.800
him with a little sailor's hat, instead of&nbsp;
a Christmas hat, since elementary school."

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:44.080
A remnant of this nautical Mr. Hankey survives&nbsp;
in a piece of obscure South Park ephemera.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:49.120
In the opening title sequence of the unaired&nbsp;
original pilot episode of South Park, Mr. Hankey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:54.160
briefly bounces across the screen, wearing a&nbsp;
primitive version of the hat. Bookending his time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:59.680
in South Park, he dons the sailor hat once again&nbsp;
when he's thrown out of town in a 2018 episode.

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:04.880
After establishing Mr. Hankey, the Christmas&nbsp;
Poo as a bit of modern seasonal storytelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:09.360
on par with The Simpsons’ long-running Treehouse&nbsp;
of Horror episodes for Halloween, South Park and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:14.800
Columbia Records capitalized on the popularity&nbsp;
of the character just two years after his debut.

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:18.160
In 1999, coupled with a TV&nbsp;
special of the same name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:21.760
Mr. Hankey's Christmas Classics hit&nbsp;
the shelves. The album features the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:26.480
Mr. Hankey theme song and other South&nbsp;
Park holiday originals, such as "Merry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:32.000
Christmas" as performed by Mr. Garrison, and&nbsp;
Kyle Broflovski's "The Lonely Jew on Christmas."

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:36.800
The novelty record proved to be a hit, ranking&nbsp;
among the top 40 best-selling holiday albums&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:41.920
of 1999, as reported by Billboard. In what&nbsp;
must have felt like an era-defining moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:46.560
Mr. Hankey's Christmas Classics ended up&nbsp;
ranking just below A Charlie Brown Christmas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:49.280
and ahead of A Christmas&nbsp;
Album, by Barbra Streisand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:54.320
whom South Park depicted as the wicked&nbsp;
colossus Mecha-Streisand in 1998.

00:06:54.320 --> 00:07:00.000
Prior to South Park's debut in 1997,&nbsp;
cable TV's most notorious animated series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:04.960
was Nickelodeon's The Ren and Stimpy Show.&nbsp;
Created by John Kricfalusi, it features the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:11.440
adventures of a rage-prone chihuahua named Ren and&nbsp;
his friend, a cat named Stimpy. In October 1997,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:17.520
Kricfalusi debuted a web series that features a&nbsp;
character named Nutty the Friendly Dump, a jovial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:22.960
talking feces log. A couple months later,&nbsp;
Mr. Hankey the Christmas Poo, another jovial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:28.960
talking feces log, made his debut on South Park,&nbsp;
and Kricfalusi called foul, telling E! Online:

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:33.200
"I got nine messages on my answering machine&nbsp;
from people who said [South Park] took like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:36.480
10 of your ideas and put them in&nbsp;
one episode. I suppose it could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:39.280
be a coincidence; everyone likes dump humor."

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:42.240
South Park co-creator Trey Parker wrote an open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:45.920
letter denying that Mr. Hankey was&nbsp;
a ripoff of Nutty the Friendly Dump.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:49.920
After explaining that Mr. Hankey was the&nbsp;
invention of his father, Parker said,

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:54.480
"It is my hope that this personal letter will put&nbsp;
an end to your attempts to slander me and my poo."

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:59.520
The gesture worked, although a few days later,&nbsp;
Kricfalusi expressed a desire for Parker to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:03.520
at the very least, admit that South&nbsp;
Park was inspired by Ren and Stimpy.

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:06.800
As South Park's modern-day&nbsp;
Christmas legend tells viewers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:12.080
Mr. Hankey lives in toilets and emerges during&nbsp;
the holidays. His magical nature is undeniable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:16.240
but could it be possible that Mr. Hankey&nbsp;
has also made his presence felt in space?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:22.400
After all, there is precedent set by the 1964&nbsp;
cult classic, Santa Claus Conquers the Martians.

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:28.160
In January 2019, NASA engineer Kevin M. Gill&nbsp;
tweeted a picture of the planet Jupiter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:33.120
captured by the Juno spacecraft the previous&nbsp;
September and then enhanced. The photo depicts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:38.240
a gigantic oblong object on the surface of the&nbsp;
largest planet in the solar system. Said shape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:43.840
is long, brown, and very closely resembles Mr.&nbsp;
Hankey, the Christmas Poo, as Gill pointed out.

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:48.000
As you might realize, this association&nbsp;
is somewhat appropriate, as Jupiter is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:50.000
after all, a gas giant.

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:54.160
According to a press release Gill sent to&nbsp;
HuffPost, "Mr. Hankey" is the unofficial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:58.800
nickname for what is technically called a "brown&nbsp;
barge" or "cyclonic region" on the planet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:03.920
What's even stranger: The picture of this Mr.&nbsp;
Hankey-esque spot on Jupiter emerged just weeks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:08.960
after South Park broadcast the episode in which&nbsp;
the Christmas character is forced into exile.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:14.080
Having been thrown off TV, Mr. Hankey apparently&nbsp;
decided to take his magic to another dimension.

